---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const fixes = [
  { cause: "One machine held the site and everything needed to run it", fix: "The site is static on Cloudflare Pages; no server of mine is in the path", status: "Done" },
  { cause: "No backup existed", fix: "restic to a home drive plus one cloud copy, done only after a tested restore", status: "Next" },
  { cause: "Nothing described the setup as code, so nothing could rebuild it", fix: "Site, CI and DNS live in one repository; DNS is Terraform", status: "Done" },
  { cause: "DNS was edited by hand and left pointing at things that no longer existed", fix: "Stale records removed; every change is now a reviewed plan with a manual apply", status: "Done" },
  { cause: "Nothing outside the machine was watching it", fix: "Upptime checks the site every five minutes from GitHub, not from my hardware", status: "Done" },
];
---
<Base
  type="article"
  title="Post-mortem: the September 2026 outage, bvrinfra.in"
  description="How bvrinfra.in went offline in September 2026, why it could not be restored, and what changed in the rebuild."
>
  <article class="post">
    <header>
      <p class="kind">Post-mortem · published 8 October 2026</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">The September 2026 outage</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In September 2026 I deleted the Azure server that ran bvrinfra.in, along with everything attached to it.
        There was no backup. The site stayed down until I rebuilt it from scratch on 8 October 2026.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="summary">
      <h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
      <dl class="summary">
        <div><dt>What broke</dt><dd>The public site, bvrinfra.in, and everything hosted on that machine</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Down from</dt><dd>September 2026 until 8 October 2026</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Data recovered</dt><dd>None from the machine. The site's code survived because it was in GitHub.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Root cause</dt><dd>A single machine with no backup and no way to rebuild it</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="what">
      <h2 id="what">What happened</h2>
      <p>
        The previous version of the site ran on one Azure virtual machine. That machine was the only place where the live configuration,
        the deployed site and everything it served existed. When I deleted it, together with its network rules and the resource group that held them,
        all of that went too.
      </p>
      <p>
        There was nothing to restore from. No backup existed, and the steps to rebuild the setup had never been written down.
        The code in GitHub described a Kubernetes stack built to serve what was mostly a static site. That setup was far more complex than the site
        needed, and it couldn't be rebuilt without the original machine.
      </p>
      <p>
        The DNS outlived the server. DNS is the set of records that tells browsers where bvrinfra.in lives.
        When I went to rebuild, it still held 22 records pointing to systems that no longer existed.
        Seventeen pointed to a deleted Cloudflare Tunnel and one to a private address on my home network; the rest pointed to the deleted machine or to a project that had ended.
        None of them was dangerous on its own, but together they described a system that was gone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="why">
      <h2 id="why">Why it couldn't be recovered</h2>
      <p>The deletion was the trigger. These were the causes.</p>
      <ol class="causes">
        <li><strong>One machine, one point of failure.</strong> The site, its runtime and its configuration all lived on the same VM.</li>
        <li><strong>No backup.</strong> Not an old one, not a partial one. My only backup script, on another machine, wrote its copies to the same disk it was protecting.</li>
        <li><strong>No infrastructure as code.</strong> Infrastructure as code means describing servers and settings in files that can rebuild them. I had none, so nothing could recreate the machine.</li>
        <li><strong>Hand-edited DNS.</strong> Records stopped matching the systems that existed, and nothing recorded why each one was there.</li>
        <li><strong>No outside monitoring.</strong> Nothing that didn't run on that machine was checking the site.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="went-well">
      <h2 id="went-well">What went right</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The domain and DNS were in a separate Cloudflare account, so the name itself was never at risk.</li>
        <li>The code was in GitHub, so I had the history of what I'd built, even if I couldn't run it.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="changed">
      <h2 id="changed">What changed</h2>
      <p>Each cause maps to a fix. The rack on the <a href="/">home page</a> shows the same list as live units.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Cause</th><th scope="col">Fix</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {fixes.map((f) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{f.cause}</td>
                <td>{f.fix}</td>
                <td><span class:list={["pill", f.status === "Done" ? "done" : "next"]}>{f.status}</span></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lesson">
      <h2 id="lesson">The lesson</h2>
      <p class="pull">
        A backup only counts once a restore from it has worked. Until then it is a hope with a schedule.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why the backups unit on the home page is the last one, and why it stays grey until a full restore has been tested.
        You can follow the rest of the rebuild in the <a href="/log/">build log</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .post { max-width: 46rem; }
  .post header { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .kind { color: var(--muted); font-stretch: 85%; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .post section { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .post h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 1.1rem + 0.8vw, 1.75rem);
    font-stretch: 92%;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .post p, .post li { line-height: 1.65; }
  .post ul, .causes { padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  .causes li, .post ul li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .post li::marker { color: var(--muted); }

  .summary { margin: 0; }
  .summary div {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9.5rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1.25rem;
    padding-block: 0.8rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .summary div:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .summary dt { color: var(--muted); font-stretch: 85%; }
  .summary dd { margin: 0; }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.95rem; min-width: 32rem; }
  th { text-align: left; font-weight: 650; color: var(--muted); font-stretch: 85%; padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem 0.6rem 0; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--rule); }
  td { vertical-align: top; padding: 0.8rem 0.75rem 0.8rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .pill {
    display: inline-block; padding: 0.15rem 0.6rem; border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 700; font-stretch: 85%; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .pill.done { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--led-live) 18%, transparent); color: var(--ink); }
  .pill.next { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--led-build) 22%, transparent); color: var(--ink); }

  .pull {
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 1.05rem + 0.8vw, 1.6rem);
    font-weight: 700; font-stretch: 95%; line-height: 1.3;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--led-live);
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }

  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .pill { border: 1px solid CanvasText; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .summary div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
  }
</style>
